Executive Summary

The optimal architecture for your requirements is Cube Core (semantic layer) + Metabase (BI engine/UI) + a thin orchestration layer (Python/Node). Neither tool alone covers the full scope, but together they provide ~80-85% of the functionality out-of-the-box. The remaining ~15-20% is the orchestration glue that wires up API-key management, routes AI prompts to the LLM, and bridges Metabase's APIs for programmatic report configuration.

Below is an evaluation of the candidate tools, followed by the recommended architecture.

---

1. Tool-by-Tool Evaluation

1.1 Cube Core (cube.dev)

What it is: An open-source, API-first semantic layer that sits between your PostgreSQL database and any consumption tool (BI frontend, custom app, AI agent).

Strengths for your use case:

· Headless & API-first: Exposes REST, GraphQL, and SQL APIs. All functionality is accessible programmatically — no GUI required.
· Manual BI support: Native support for aggregations, grouping, date filters, and joins through its data schema (cubes, dimensions, measures).
· AI context provider: Cube’s semantic layer can feed schema/context to LLMs, enabling natural-language-to-SQL generation.
· Exportability: Query results are returned as structured JSON; raw data can be exported to CSV with minimal effort.
· Self-hosted & free: MIT-licensed; runs in Docker; no usage limits on the open-source core.

Weaknesses:

· No built-in visualization or dashboarding UI. Cube Core is purely a backend; you must supply your own frontend for charts/dashboards.
· No built-in AI assistant. AI integration must be built by you (though Cube provides LangChain integration and MCP servers to accelerate this).

Verdict: Cube Core is the ideal backend for your headless reporting engine. It ticks every box except visualization and AI orchestration.

---

1.2 Metabase (Open-Source Edition)

What it is: A full-stack BI platform with a polished UI for non-technical users, plus a REST API for programmatic control.

Strengths for your use case:

· Manual BI out-of-the-box: Drag-and-drop query builder, aggregations, group-by, date filters, joins, and 20+ visualization types — all without writing SQL.
· AI features (recently open-sourced): Metabase 60+ includes natural language querying (Metabot), SQL generation, and an Agent API. Users can bring their own LLM API key.
· Exportability: Full support for CSV, JSON, Excel, PNG, and PDF exports; dashboard-level export to PDF; query history and state saving are built in.
· Self-hosted & free: AGPL-licensed; runs in a single Docker container; no user/seat limits in the OSS edition.
· REST API: Programmatic access to dashboards, questions, collections, and permissions.

Weaknesses:

· API completeness issues: The Metabase API was not designed as a first-class headless interface. Certain operations (e.g., converting questions to models, complex report creation) are difficult or impossible via the API.
· Embedding limitations: Full app embedding (interactive embedding) requires a paid license in some configurations. However, static embedding and public links are free.
· AI depends on Metabase's semantic layer: The AI features work against Metabase's own data model, not Cube's. If you use Cube as your semantic layer, Metabase's AI may not fully leverage it.

Verdict: Metabase is the best frontend BI UI available in open source. Its API is sufficient for basic automation but not for a fully headless architecture. The AI features (BYO-key, natural language queries) directly address your "AI as Edit/Assist" requirement.

---

1.3 DynamicJasper

What it is: A Java library that simplifies the creation of JasperReports templates programmatically.

Why it doesn't fit:

· Not a BI platform. It's a report-generation library for PDF/print-ready output. No interactive dashboards, no ad-hoc querying, no AI integration.
· No API-first headless architecture. It's a code library, not a service.
· Heavy lifting required. You would need to build the entire query engine, visualization layer, and API wrapper yourself.

Verdict: Inappropriate for your use case. Avoid.

---

1.4 Alternative Worth Considering: Lightdash

What it is: An open-source BI platform built on top of dbt (data build tool). It offers a semantic layer, visualizations, and AI-powered analytics.

Strengths:

· dbt-native: If your team already uses dbt for data transformations, Lightdash integrates seamlessly.
· AI agents: Custom AI analysts can answer natural language questions.
· Headless API: REST API for programmatic access.

Why it's not the top pick:

· dbt dependency: Lightdash assumes a dbt project exists. Without dbt, you must define metrics in YAML, which adds setup complexity.
· Maturity: Smaller community and less mature API compared to Cube/Metabase.

Verdict: A viable alternative if you already use dbt. Otherwise, Cube + Metabase is simpler and more mature.

