3. Why This Architecture Hits 90% Out-of-the-Box

Requirement How It's Met
API-First & Headless Cube Core is natively headless with 3 API protocols. Metabase adds a UI but is optional; the orchestration layer can talk to both Cube and Metabase APIs programmatically.
Manual BI (aggregations, group-by, joins, date filters) Cube's data schema defines all these operations; Metabase's query builder exposes them visually.
AI "Edit/Assist" The orchestration layer sends Cube's schema + user prompt to the LLM, receives a Cube query or Metabase configuration, and applies it via API. Metabase's own Metabot can also be used for natural language chart creation.
Export to CSV Metabase supports CSV export natively; Cube API returns JSON that can be converted to CSV trivially.
Visualizations & State Saving Metabase provides 20+ chart types, dashboards, and persisted query history.
Self-Hosted & Single-Tenant Both Cube Core and Metabase OSS run in Docker on the user's infrastructure.
FOSS Licensing Cube Core: MIT. Metabase OSS: AGPL. Orchestration layer: your own code (MIT).
Running Costs Only server hosting + user's own LLM API usage. No per-seat or per-query fees.

---

4. What You'll Need to Build (The 10%)

The orchestration layer handles three tasks:

1. API Key Management: Securely store the user's LLM API key and inject it into prompt calls.
2. AI-to-Cube Bridge: Retrieve the Cube data schema (via Cube's /meta API), format it as context, and send it alongside the user's natural language prompt to the LLM. Parse the LLM's response into a Cube query (JSON) or Metabase API call.
3. AI-to-Metabase Bridge: Use Metabase's API to programmatically create or update questions, dashboards, or collections based on AI-generated configurations. This is the trickiest part because Metabase's API has gaps; you may need to fall back to direct Cube SQL queries for complex operations.

A reference implementation in Python would be approximately 500–800 lines using FastAPI or Flask.

